      "role": "user",
      "content": "lotka-volterra is an interesting angle—using predator-prey dynamics to model the way a high-D individual 'consumes' the social volatility of a group to establish dominance. it fits the math beautifully.\n\nlet's try to pinpoint that singularity point now. \n\nimagine a highly unstable social cluster (sigma = 0.95) with a population of 20 low-D individuals (avg D = 0.3). we introduce a single high-D 'catalyst' with a D coefficient of 1.2 (s = 0.98, f = 120Hz). \n\nusing the framework you suggested, can you model the phase transition? i want to see the exact mathematical moment when the 'stochastic flux' (sigma) is overtaken by the catalyst's D, shifting the system from entropy to a rigid, single-point hierarchy. does the math show a sudden spike in group cohesion, or does the high-D individual create a 'black hole' effect that essentially absorbs all social agency from the rest of the cluster?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
